<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">5675436</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2138544</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>PHENOTYPIC EXPRESSIONS OF THE MAJOR HISTOCOMPATIBILITY LOCUS IN MAN (<italic>HL-A</italic>): LEUKOCYTE ANTIGENS AND MIXED LEUKOCYTE CULTURE REACTIVITY</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Amos</surname><given-names>D. Bernard</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Bach</surname><given-names>Fritz H.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x32f1a60N0x3458450">From the Division of Immunology, Duke University, Durham, North Carolina 27706, and the Departments of Medical Genetics and Medicine, University of Wisconsin, Madison, Wisconsin 53706</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>10</month><year>1968</year></pub-date><volume>128</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>623</fpage><lpage>637</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>29</day><month>5</month><year>1968</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1968 by The Rockefeller University Press</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> The evidence is reviewed that a single genetic system, the major histocompatibility locus in man, <italic>HL-A</italic>, determines most of the antigens measured by presently available leukocyte isoantisera, and also controls reactivity in one-way mixed leucocyte culture tests. Studies in 12 families are presented to support this conclusion. Some interesting exceptions to the general typing&#x02014;MLC tests correlation are presented and discussed. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>